A||N||7||KRMFiero||Turbo 4.5L?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||02-20-2001||12:08 PM||||I have seen this for sale for a while: <BR>----------------------------<BR>Air Research Turbo Unit  w. waste gate for  T-Bird 2.3L, commonly used to pump up the 2.8 V6 in Fieros $125<BR>----------------------------<BR>What would i have to do to get this to work on My 4.5L Caddy engine.  Would it be better to install it after the engine is in or before it is in   or would it be better not to do it at all?     Does it seem like the turbo comes with everything? If it will not work on my Fiero would it work on my friends Eclipse or on a 4.0L jeep?<P><BR>Kyle<BR><P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||02-20-2001||12:44 PM||||Well I am not a turbo expert but I can say that a turbo made for a 2.3L engine will spool up on a 4.5L way early, simply beacuse of the more exhaust gases made by the lager motor.  Maybe two of those turbos they add up to 4.6 L now you are in the ball park at least, though I can say for sure||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||02-20-2001||05:18 PM||||I think Hank is on the right track. Of course, there is some flexibility in sizing a turbo by using different size base plates. Also, on a 4.5 using a single turbo, you'll have to run a crossover pipe and form a single exhaust (like the v6 Fiero) to route through your turbo. Here are a couple of links that may help you decide how to go about designing your own system: <A HREF="http://www.sdsefi.com/techmods.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sdsefi.com/techmods.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> <P><BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||billyboy||02-21-2001||02:18 AM||||You'd be better off buying a GMC Cyclone or Typhoon 4.7L.  It's cheaper, easier for installation, and you won't have NEAR the headaches of a custom turbo install on a V8.  On turbocharging a V8 Firebird (I've had experience with this) it took over 1200 man hours (yeah, pay a shop $40/hr for that!), required 2 custom exhaust and intake manifolds ($600), mandrel bent tubing from the turbo to the intercooler, then from the intercooler to the intake, and a mandrel bent exhaust ($1100 for all the pipes), new ECU ($1800), and a butt-load of tuning (about 60hrs on a DynoJet @$20/hr) to get it tuned right.  On top of all that, you've got little petty stuff, like bigger injectors, bigger fuel pump, upgraded ignition system, any tranny upgrades (Level 10 charged us $2200 for our performance rebuild), boost controllers, additional injector controllers, turbo timers, lower compression pistons, hardened and shot peened rods, bigger oil pump, etc. etc. etc.  I could go on for hours.  Trust me man, you can't just "throw on" a turbo.  It's more trouble than it's worth, unless you want extremely high horsepower and have unlimited availability of a shop that will do this for you... and an endless amount of cash.  The Firebird had over $8900 tied up in that when it was all said and done, and only had another 120hp.  You can get that with a 100 shot of NOS.<P>My advice: if you want a turbo, sell the Caddy motor and get a 4.7L Cyclone/Typhoon motor and worry about that fabrication rather than turbocharging the Caddy motor, it's going to be much easier and cheaper.  If you're just looking for power, beef up that Caddy motor, TAD has high comp pistons, higher velocity heads, and all kinds of cool stuff.  Good luck finding them though... I've been looking since I heard about them and still can't find them (Tim Allen Designs).  I think Edlebrock made some of that stuff too.  <P>Hope this helps you out!<P>Billyboy<BR>||||reg||2
Z||000004||KRMFiero||02-21-2001||12:11 PM||||As much as i love my Fiero its not worth spending like 5000$ for a turbo... i'll probley just go with the NOS heheh<P><BR>thanx<BR>Kyle<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||lou_dias||02-21-2001||12:30 PM||||It sounds like there 6 cylinder package could hook up to the crank sensor in the 3.4 camaro engines and be used to run that.  Too bad it's still batch fire.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.sdsefi.com/6f.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sdsefi.com/6f.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-21-2001||01:21 PM||||The SFI engines go batch above 2500-3000 RPM anyway. SFI is only for idle/low speed quality. I'd never turn down a system because it's batch fire. Hell the 91-93 DOHC's aren't even batch. They are "all at once".||||reg||1
Z||000007||lou_dias||02-21-2001||03:28 PM||||Wasn't it in 94 when they gained 5 hp?<BR>Anyway, I want SFI for my primary driving experience to save me fuel and make my car more responsive.  Otherwise what do I have to gain over MFI...well I guess DIS is nice.||||reg||1
